# Seeing probability and false positives

This lab makes an abstract topic visible. Read one short section, guess what the
next result or picture will show, and then run the code. It is fine if the words
`alpha`, `z-score`, or `critical value` are unfamiliar; each is introduced only
when the picture needs it.

All measurements are **synthetic teaching data**. BioLang calculates every
number and shaded curve, so you can change a value and see the result update.

Run the whole notebook from the repository root:

`target/release/bl notebook books/biostatistics/book/src/downloads/normal-distribution-lab.bln`

Or export an executed HTML page:

`target/release/bl notebook books/biostatistics/book/src/downloads/normal-distribution-lab.bln --export html`

The lesson follows the normal-distribution topic sequence in `BioStats.pdf`,
but explains it independently in executable BioLang code. Expressions are
written explicitly and checked in code.

## Learning goals

By the end, you should be able to explain in your own words:

- why probability is an area, not the height of a curve at one point;
- how far a measurement is from a reference mean;
- how BioLang finds an area or the cutoff that gives a chosen area;
- why a one-sided question and a two-sided question use different cutoffs;
- why a p-value is calculated from the observed result; and
- why a 5% false-positive rate does not make each conclusion "95% certain."

## Module 1: the total probability space

A standard normal random variable has mean 0 and standard deviation 1. Its
curve is a **density**: the height at one point is not a probability. Probability
is represented by an **area over an interval**, and the complete area is 1.

BioLang provides three complementary normal-distribution functions:

- `dnorm(z)`: How high is the density curve at `z`?
- `pnorm(z)`: What probability lies to the left of `z`?
- `qnorm(p)`: Which `z` has probability `p` to its left?

```biolang
let density_at_zero = dnorm(0.0)
let area_left_of_zero = pnorm(0.0)
let median_z = qnorm(0.5)

println("density at z = 0:       " + str(round(density_at_zero, 4)))
println("area left of z = 0:     " + str(round(area_left_of_zero, 4)))
println("z with 50% to its left: " + str(round(median_z, 4)))

if abs(area_left_of_zero - 0.5) > 0.000001 {
    error("standard-normal symmetry check failed")
}
```

Expected insight: `dnorm(0)` is about 0.3989, but `pnorm(0)` is 0.5. A density
height can exceed 1 for sufficiently narrow distributions; an area probability
cannot.

The next hidden helper builds the lesson's SVG figures. It uses `dnorm` for the
curve and `qnorm` for every boundary, so the picture and the reported numbers
cannot quietly disagree.

```biolang
# @hide-code
fn _lab_x(z) { round(55.0 + (z + 4.0) * 650.0 / 8.0, 2) }
fn _lab_y(z) { round(280.0 - dnorm(z) * 555.0, 2) }

fn _lab_point(z) {
    str(_lab_x(z)) + "," + str(_lab_y(z))
}

fn _lab_curve_points(xs) {
    xs |> map(|z| _lab_point(z)) |> join(" ")
}

fn _lab_polygon(xs, colour) {
    if len(xs) == 0 { return "" }
    let first_x = _lab_x(xs[0])
    let last_x = _lab_x(xs[len(xs) - 1])
    "<polygon points=\"" + str(first_x) + ",280 " +
        _lab_curve_points(xs) + " " + str(last_x) +
        ",280\" fill=\"" + colour + "\" opacity=\"0.65\"/>"
}

fn _lab_cut_line(z, label) {
    let x = _lab_x(z)
    let box_x = round(x - 50.0, 2)
    "<line x1=\"" + str(x) + "\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"" + str(x) +
        "\" y2=\"280\" stroke=\"#b91c1c\" stroke-width=\"2\" " +
        "stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/>" +
        "<rect x=\"" + str(box_x) + "\" y=\"48\" width=\"100\" height=\"24\" " +
        "rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff7ed\" stroke=\"#fecaca\"/>" +
        "<text x=\"" + str(x) + "\" y=\"65\" text-anchor=\"middle\" " +
        "font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#991b1b\">" +
        label + "</text>"
}

fn _lab_normal_svg(title, alpha, tail) {
    let xs = range(0, 321) |> map(|i| -4.0 + float(i) * 8.0 / 320.0)
    let curve = _lab_curve_points(xs)
    let left_cut = if tail == "left" { qnorm(alpha) } else {
        if tail == "two" { qnorm(alpha / 2.0) } else { -9.0 }
    }
    let right_cut = if tail == "right" { qnorm(1.0 - alpha) } else {
        if tail == "two" { qnorm(1.0 - alpha / 2.0) } else { 9.0 }
    }
    let left_xs = xs |> filter(|z| z <= left_cut)
    let right_xs = xs |> filter(|z| z >= right_cut)
    let left_fill = _lab_polygon(left_xs, "#f87171")
    let right_fill = _lab_polygon(right_xs, "#f87171")
    let left_line = if left_cut > -8.0 {
        _lab_cut_line(left_cut, "z = " + str(round(left_cut, 3)))
    } else { "" }
    let right_line = if right_cut < 8.0 {
        _lab_cut_line(right_cut, "z = " + str(round(right_cut, 3)))
    } else { "" }
    let ticks = [-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3] |> map(|z| {
        let x = _lab_x(float(z))
        "<line x1=\"" + str(x) + "\" y1=\"280\" x2=\"" + str(x) +
            "\" y2=\"286\" stroke=\"#475569\"/>" +
            "<text x=\"" + str(x) + "\" y=\"302\" text-anchor=\"middle\" " +
            "font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#475569\">" +
            str(z) + "</text>"
    }) |> join("")
    "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"760\" height=\"350\" " +
        "viewBox=\"0 0 760 350\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"" + title + "\">" +
        "<rect width=\"760\" height=\"350\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\"/>" +
        "<text x=\"380\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" " +
        "font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">" + title + "</text>" +
        "<line x1=\"55\" y1=\"280\" x2=\"705\" y2=\"280\" stroke=\"#475569\"/>" +
        left_fill + right_fill +
        "<polyline points=\"" + curve + "\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0f172a\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>" +
        left_line + right_line + ticks +
        "<text x=\"380\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" " +
        "font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#334155\">standard-normal z-score</text></svg>"
}
```

```biolang
println(_lab_normal_svg("The complete standard-normal probability space", 0.05, "none"))
```

### A visual symmetry check

The probability between `-1` and `1` is the cumulative area to the left of 1
minus the cumulative area to the left of -1.

```biolang
let within_one_sd = pnorm(1.0) - pnorm(-1.0)
let within_two_sd = pnorm(2.0) - pnorm(-2.0)
let within_three_sd = pnorm(3.0) - pnorm(-3.0)

println("P(-1 <= Z <= 1) = " + str(round(within_one_sd, 4)))
println("P(-2 <= Z <= 2) = " + str(round(within_two_sd, 4)))
println("P(-3 <= Z <= 3) = " + str(round(within_three_sd, 4)))
```

These are the familiar 68%, 95%, and 99.7% landmarks. They are descriptive
properties of the normal distribution, not automatic thresholds for every
scientific decision.

## Bridge: turn a biological measurement into a z-score

A z-score tells you how many standard deviations a value lies from a reference
mean:

`z = (observed - reference mean) / reference standard deviation`

Suppose a healthy reference assay has mean 100 units and standard deviation 15.
A specimen measures 130 units.

Before running the cell, predict whether the z-score is 1, 2, or 3.

```biolang
let reference_mean = 100.0
let reference_sd = 15.0
let observed_value = 130.0
let observed_z = (observed_value - reference_mean) / reference_sd
let percentile = pnorm(observed_z)

println("z-score:                  " + str(round(observed_z, 3)))
println("proportion below specimen: " + str(round(percentile, 4)))
println("proportion above specimen: " + str(round(1.0 - percentile, 4)))
```

The observation is 2 standard deviations above the reference mean. Under this
reference model, only about 2.3% of observations lie above it.

## Module 2: choose alpha and place the risk

The significance level `alpha` is chosen **before looking at the result**. It is
the long-run probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when the null is
true, assuming the model and testing procedure are valid.

It is not:

- the probability that the null hypothesis is true;
- the probability that this particular result is a false positive; or
- a measure of biological importance.

### Direction determines where alpha goes

- A **right-tailed** alternative places all alpha in the upper tail.
- A **left-tailed** alternative places all alpha in the lower tail.
- A **two-tailed** alternative divides alpha equally between both tails.

```biolang
let alpha = 0.05
let right_critical = qnorm(1.0 - alpha)
let left_critical = qnorm(alpha)
let two_critical = qnorm(1.0 - alpha / 2.0)

println("right-tailed alpha 0.05: z >= " + str(round(right_critical, 4)))
println("left-tailed alpha 0.05:  z <= " + str(round(left_critical, 4)))
println("two-tailed alpha 0.05:   |z| >= " + str(round(two_critical, 4)))

if abs(right_critical + left_critical) > 0.000001 {
    error("symmetric critical values did not mirror each other")
}
```

```biolang
println(_lab_normal_svg("Right-tailed test: alpha = 0.05", 0.05, "right"))
```

```biolang
println(_lab_normal_svg("Two-tailed test: alpha = 0.05", 0.05, "two"))
```

## Module 3: understand a cumulative Z-table

A traditional cumulative Z-table is a printed approximation to `pnorm(z)`. Its
body answers: **what area lies to the left of this z-score?** The row normally
gives the first decimal place and the column gives the second.

```biolang
let z_table_demo = [1.64, 1.65, 1.96, -2.33] |> map(|z| {
    {z: z, cumulative_left_area: round(pnorm(z), 4)}
})

println(table(z_table_demo))
```

For a right-tailed test with `alpha = 0.05`, the target left area is `0.95`.
The table values at 1.64 and 1.65 straddle it, so printed tables report a cutoff
near 1.645. `qnorm(0.95)` performs the inverse lookup directly.

```biolang
let target_left_area = 0.95
let exact_lookup = qnorm(target_left_area)
println("qnorm(0.95) = " + str(round(exact_lookup, 6)))
println("pnorm(answer) = " + str(round(pnorm(exact_lookup), 6)))
```

The inverse check should return 0.95. Keep the table skill for reading papers
and exams; use `qnorm` in analysis so arbitrary alpha values do not require
rounding by eye.

## Module 4: work the three critical-value examples

### Example A: right-tailed test

In this synthetic teaching question, only a **higher** value matches the
pre-chosen direction. The direction was set before looking at the result, and
`alpha = 0.05`.

```biolang
let example_a_alpha = 0.05
let example_a_cutoff = qnorm(1.0 - example_a_alpha)
println("target cumulative left area: " + str(1.0 - example_a_alpha))
println("reject H0 when z >= " + str(round(example_a_cutoff, 3)))
```

Answer: the cutoff is approximately `+1.645`.

### Example B: two-tailed test

An assay may drift either high or low. With `alpha = 0.05`, each tail receives
`0.025`, leaving 0.95 in the center.

```biolang
let example_b_alpha = 0.05
let area_per_tail = example_b_alpha / 2.0
let example_b_upper = qnorm(1.0 - area_per_tail)
let example_b_lower = qnorm(area_per_tail)

println("area per tail: " + str(area_per_tail))
println("lower cutoff: " + str(round(example_b_lower, 3)))
println("upper cutoff: " + str(round(example_b_upper, 3)))
```

Answer: reject beyond approximately `-1.96` or `+1.96`.

### Example C: left-tailed test

In another synthetic question, success means a value **lower** than a reference
level. For `alpha = 0.01`, the cumulative left area is alpha itself.

```biolang
let example_c_alpha = 0.01
let example_c_cutoff = qnorm(example_c_alpha)
println("reject H0 when z <= " + str(round(example_c_cutoff, 3)))
```

```biolang
println(_lab_normal_svg("Left-tailed test: alpha = 0.01", 0.01, "left"))
```

Answer: the cutoff is approximately `-2.326`, commonly rounded to `-2.33` in a
Z-table.

## Critical value versus p-value

The critical value is fixed by the design before observing the data. The
p-value is calculated afterward from the observed test statistic.

For an observed `z = 2.4`:

```biolang
let result_z = 2.4
let right_p = 1.0 - pnorm(result_z)
let two_sided_p = 2.0 * (1.0 - pnorm(abs(result_z)))

println("right-tailed p-value: " + str(round(right_p, 6)))
println("two-sided p-value:    " + str(round(two_sided_p, 6)))
println("right-tailed decision at alpha 0.05: " +
    if right_p < 0.05 { "reject H0" } else { "fail to reject H0" })
```

Do not say “accept the null.” A non-significant test can mean no important
effect, noisy measurements, too few replicates, or a poor design. It says the
data did not cross the chosen evidential threshold under the stated model.

## See alpha happen: a false-positive simulation

If the null hypothesis is true and we repeat a valid two-sided test many times
at `alpha = 0.05`, about 5% of results should fall outside `±1.96` merely through
sampling variation.

Before running the cell, predict whether the count will be exactly 500.

```biolang
set_seed(20260809)
let repetitions = 10000
let null_z_scores = rnorm(repetitions, 0.0, 1.0)
let null_cutoff = qnorm(0.975)
let false_positives = null_z_scores
    |> filter(|z| abs(z) >= null_cutoff)
    |> len()
let false_positive_rate = float(false_positives) / float(repetitions)

println("false positives: " + str(false_positives) + " / " + str(repetitions))
println("observed rate:   " + str(round(false_positive_rate, 4)))

if false_positive_rate < 0.035 or false_positive_rate > 0.065 {
    error("simulation landed implausibly far from alpha; inspect the RNG or test")
}
```

It should be near 5%, but not exactly 5%. Alpha describes repeated procedures;
it does not label any individual rejected result as 95% certain.

## Confidence intervals use the same boundary

For a two-sided test at alpha 0.05, the central probability is 0.95 and the
normal critical value is `qnorm(0.975)`. That same multiplier appears in a 95%
normal confidence interval:

`estimate ± critical value × standard error`

```biolang
let estimate = 4.2
let standard_error = 1.5
let ci_z = qnorm(0.975)
let ci_lower = estimate - ci_z * standard_error
let ci_upper = estimate + ci_z * standard_error

println("estimate: " + str(estimate))
println("95% normal CI: [" + str(round(ci_lower, 3)) + ", " +
    str(round(ci_upper, 3)) + "]")
println("does the interval exclude zero? " + str(ci_lower > 0.0 or ci_upper < 0.0))
```

The interval and corresponding two-sided test agree about whether zero is
excluded, provided they use the same model and assumptions. In small samples,
use an appropriate t critical value rather than automatically using 1.96.

## Retrieval practice

Try answering each question before revealing the executable check.

- **1.** What cumulative left area gives a right-tailed alpha of 0.02?
- **2.** What cumulative left area gives the upper boundary of a two-tailed
  alpha of 0.02?
- **3.** If `z = -2.1`, what is the left-tailed p-value?
- **4.** If the question was “different” rather than “lower,” what is the
  two-sided p-value?

```biolang
let review_alpha = 0.02
let review_right_area = 1.0 - review_alpha
let review_two_upper_area = 1.0 - review_alpha / 2.0
let review_z = -2.1
let review_left_p = pnorm(review_z)
let review_two_p = 2.0 * pnorm(-abs(review_z))

println("1. right-tail target area: " + str(review_right_area))
println("2. two-tail upper area:    " + str(review_two_upper_area))
println("3. left-tailed p-value:    " + str(round(review_left_p, 6)))
println("4. two-sided p-value:      " + str(round(review_two_p, 6)))

if abs(pnorm(qnorm(review_right_area)) - review_right_area) > 0.000001 {
    error("inverse-CDF review check failed")
}
```

## Decision recipe

When facing a new problem:

- **1.** State the null and alternative in biological language.
- **2.** Decide the direction before seeing the data.
- **3.** Choose alpha and justify its consequences.
- **4.** Verify that the test model and assumptions fit the design.
- **5.** Compute the statistic and p-value.
- **6.** Report the estimate, uncertainty, effect size, and decision together.
- **7.** Never use a matching threshold as a substitute for biological
  importance.

Continue with Days 3, 4, 6, and 7 of *Practical Biostatistics in 30 Days* for
distributions, probability, confidence intervals, and complete hypothesis
testing.
